{"question_id": "caf03d32", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Since you only recently started using the slow cooker — and your beef stew already turned out delicious — a few pointers tailored to where you are:\n\n- **Build on the stew win.** The same low-and-slow approach works for the other recipes I suggested: chili con carne, pulled pork, pot roast, Korean BBQ beef, butter chicken, chicken tacos, mac and cheese, and breakfast casserole.\n- **Lean into your plant-based shift.** From the vegetarian/vegan lists, good next tries: lentil curry, roasted vegetable stew, black bean chili, eggplant parmesan, butternut squash risotto, vegan chili, quinoa and black bean bowl, lentil and mushroom stew, and chickpea and spinach curry.\n- **For your cashew yogurt project:** use a thermometer if you can (180–185°F matters for killing unwanted bacteria and denaturing proteins, though simmering bubbles work as a fallback), use a vegan yogurt starter or probiotic powder designed for plant-based yogurts, and adjust the cashew-to-water ratio — less water for thicker yogurt, longer blending for thinner — then chill at least 2 hours so it sets.\n- **For smoothness,** strain the blended cashew base: cheesecloth filters best but is messy, nut milk bags are made for plant milks but pricier, and a fine-mesh sieve is accessible but may leave slight graininess.\n\nIf a specific dish is giving you trouble, tell me which one and I can get more specific.", "n_records": 20, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 3324, "completion_tokens": 1767, "calls": 2}, "selected": ["self|slow_cooker_skill", "self|beef_stew_made", "self|recipe_interest", "self|plant_based_diet", "self|yogurt_making_interest", "self|thermometer_question", "self|cashew_yogurt_plan", "self|straining_question", "assistant|slow_cooker_recipe_recommendations", "assistant|yogurt_recipe", "assistant|yogurt_temperature_advice", "assistant|vegetarian_recipe_recommendations", "assistant|vegan_recipe_recommendations", "ass